## 4. InventoryExceptionHandler.kt

**Role:** this is where the service's named error becomes an HTTP response. The exception is thrown in the service, passes *untouched* through the controller, and gets caught here.

```kotlin
// Why: RestController advice so spring will catch and return specific responses we have built out for every controller rather than us building out multiple response entities for each controller. We rejected ResponseStatusException because its body is JSON, not text/plain.

/** Where the various custom built exceptions are transformed to HTTP responses. */
@RestControllerAdvice
class InventoryExceptionHandler{

    // Contract: paths./inventory/{skuId}.get - 404 with text/plain when SkuNotFoundException is thrown.

    /** Returns a 404 text/plain response whose body is the exception's message. */
    @ExceptionHandler(SkuNotFoundException::class)
    fun handleSkuNotFound(ex: SkuNotFoundException ): ResponseEntity<String> =

        ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
            .contentType(MediaType.TEXT_PLAIN)
            .body(ex.message ?: "SKU not found.")
}
```
[InventoryExceptionHandler.kt:11-26](src/main/kotlin/com/robertwoolbright/inventory/inventory/error/InventoryExceptionHandler.kt#L11-L26)

**Your turn:**
- What do `@RestControllerAdvice` and `@ExceptionHandler(SkuNotFoundException::class)` each do? How does Spring know to call `handleSkuNotFound`, when nothing in your code ever calls it?
- Why return a `ResponseEntity<String>` here instead of just a `String`, the way the controller returns a plain `InventoryItem`?
- There's an Elvis again: `ex.message ?: "SKU not found."`. Why is it needed, when `SkuNotFoundException` always builds a message?

